WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:14.840
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey, everyone.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.690
Welcome to "Eyes on
Enterprise," where

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:19.060
I'm bringing on Googlers to
talk about the technology

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.520
landscapes that are
helping enterprises scale,

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.960
adapt, and modernize.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.640
My name is Stephanie
Wong, and today I

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.300
have Sona Oakley, Solution
Architect here at Google Cloud.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.380
Very important topic that
we're covering today--

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.439
we're talking about
migration to the Cloud

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:34.690
and diving into
strategies and the tools

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:35.723
to help you get there.

00:00:35.723 --> 00:00:37.640
There's a lot that we're
going to cover today,

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.550
so I want to thank you so
much for being on the show.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:40.150
SONA OAKLEY: Absolutely.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:41.483
Thank you so much for having me.

00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:43.360
I'm really excited
to come on the show

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:44.740
and talk about migrations.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.140
STEPHANIE WONG:
Great, so huge topic.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.090
First off, migration
is something

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:52.052
that people often consider
for a long period of time

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:54.010
because they have rightful
concerns over moving

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.572
their workloads to the Cloud.

00:00:55.572 --> 00:00:57.280
And then, on top of
that, small companies

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.150
versus large companies have
different needs and migration

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:00.650
paths.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.482
So how do you start to
dissect that process?

00:01:03.482 --> 00:01:04.690
SONA OAKLEY: Yes, absolutely.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.232
I would say that the first thing
that you want to start with,

00:01:07.232 --> 00:01:10.192
because migrations is
such a heavy topic,

00:01:10.192 --> 00:01:11.650
is, you want to
start with the why.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.720
Ask yourself the question,
why are we going down

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.977
this path, what is going to
be the business goal that I'm

00:01:16.977 --> 00:01:19.060
accomplishing, or even the
technical goal that I'm

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.110
accomplishing.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:23.090
And once you kind of frame
that and keep that in mind,

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:25.030
it really makes it
clear how we can

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:27.730
get from where your
company is today to where

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:28.840
it is that you want to be.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:32.140
And whether that's being able to
develop faster, whether that's

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.810
being able to take advantage
of all of the efficiencies

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.170
and scale that moving
to the Cloud can bring,

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.250
it'll really vary
based on your business

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:42.033
and how big your business is,
what your business goals are.

00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:43.450
STEPHANIE WONG:
So there are a lot

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.430
of ways to move to the
Cloud and along with it

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:48.135
many considerations when
you're picking a strategy.

00:01:48.135 --> 00:01:51.130
You know, some companies want
to decommission some workloads.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.630
They want to consolidate.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.550
Others want to move
their VM to the Cloud.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:57.340
And others want to
modernize by transforming

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:58.480
into the containers.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.060
So how do you start in that
decision-making process?

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:01.930
SONA OAKLEY:
Essentially, the way

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:02.890
that you would want
to think about it

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.932
is that once we have that
North Star that we want

00:02:04.932 --> 00:02:07.420
to accomplish, we want
to go back to square one

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:10.030
and really start to
build a catalog of all

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:12.033
of the applications that
you have, the workloads

00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:13.450
that you're thinking
about moving,

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:16.120
what your networking and
security requirements are.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.983
And once we've categorized
and built out that list,

00:02:18.983 --> 00:02:21.400
we can start to think about
what our migration strategy is

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:22.540
moving forward.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.480
Now, when I say that, a lot
of people think in their minds

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.550
that they need a very
complicated system

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:30.568
diagram, where there's all
these connections taking place--

00:02:30.568 --> 00:02:32.860
you know, where we have the
exact schematics of the way

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:34.480
that our applications work.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.340
For larger enterprises
and businesses

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.320
that have grown organically,
sometimes that's not feasible.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.230
So I would say that
coming to the table

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:43.900
with anything, even if
it's just a napkin, which

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:44.890
has happened to me--

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:46.360
a customer showed
up with a napkin

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:49.383
of just all their applications
and workloads written down

00:02:49.383 --> 00:02:50.800
that they wrote
down on the flight

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.600
to come up to Mountain View.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.420
So I would say that even
something that simple

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.280
gives us a good starting
point and gives us

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.070
an idea of at least what
is top-of-mind for you.

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:01.820
And then we can go
through and dig through

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:04.180
with the other various
groups and lines of business

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:08.140
that you have to figure out what
are other applications, what

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:10.030
are other resources
that are being used.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:11.140
STEPHANIE WONG: So going
back to the first part

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:13.450
of the workflow-- collecting
inventory-- it can include

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:16.580
a lot of things like
dependencies on your app stack,

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.820
including your database
and message brokers.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:21.220
And then you have your
infrastructure underlying it,

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:23.563
firewall rules,
security policies.

00:03:23.563 --> 00:03:24.730
Am I missing anything there?

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.470
SONA OAKLEY: I mean, that
really covers the meat of it.

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:29.980
And then things like
source code repositories--

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:32.740
oftentimes, the
gotchas happen when

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:34.280
a business grows organically.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.330
And we see people coming
out of the woodwork

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:38.980
as this migration
process is happening.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.290
Hey, actually I'm keeping my
source code over here and not

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:42.190
in the official repository.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:42.610
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:44.652
SONA OAKLEY: Or if I've
got this machine that I'm

00:03:44.652 --> 00:03:48.470
using 2% of CPU on for, you
know, my own workloads-- things

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:48.970
like that.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:51.220
STEPHANIE WONG: Comprehensive
evaluation ahead of time

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:52.792
is going to save
you in the long run.

00:03:52.792 --> 00:03:53.500
SONA OAKLEY: Yes.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:54.460
STEPHANIE WONG: So
I know that there

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:56.740
are a lot of cases where
moving to the Cloud

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:59.440
may not be practical,
or it's just not

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.210
technically feasible
in the near-term.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:02.680
So you know, like,
for example, you

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.840
have licenses that you
can't move to the Cloud

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:08.350
or your tech stack may not be
virtualizable in some cases

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:10.620
or you have third-party
frameworks and languages being

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:11.120
used.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:12.915
So what do you do
in those cases?

00:04:12.915 --> 00:04:15.040
SONA OAKLEY: Yeah, I would
say that in those cases,

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:16.399
it's OK to say no.

00:04:16.399 --> 00:04:18.070
And sometimes, no
is the right answer.

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:20.690
For example, you know,
mainframes are not going away.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:23.230
We've predicted that they
will be for the past 40 years.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:25.260
And they're here to stay.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:26.920
So if you have legacy
applications that

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.230
are running in those
kinds of environments,

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:31.150
it's totally fine to
leave them where they are

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:33.310
and then to focus on
what we can accomplish

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:34.810
because the last
thing we want to do

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:38.170
is kind of get into a situation
where we're trying to force

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:39.760
a square peg into round hole.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:41.230
And we can also do
something that's

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:43.567
a close approximation
to moving to Cloud.

00:04:43.567 --> 00:04:45.400
So for example, I was
working with a company

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:49.210
that was getting close to
a data center shut down.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.220
And so instead of
having them move over

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:53.050
completely to the
Cloud, what we did was,

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.258
we moved them to a
co-location facility.

00:04:55.258 --> 00:04:56.800
And what that allowed
them to do was,

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:59.440
it allowed them to still
reap some of the benefits

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:04.180
because they're still located
close to a Cloud entry point

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:06.140
and are able to get
that high throughput,

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:09.040
low latency that they were
looking for without doing

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:10.348
the official Cloud migration.

00:05:10.348 --> 00:05:12.640
STEPHANIE WONG: So just
jumping off what you just said,

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.348
there are a lot of
ways to approach this.

00:05:14.348 --> 00:05:16.380
And you have to meet
customers where they are.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:18.575
And you know, some may want
to do an all-in-one Lift

00:05:18.575 --> 00:05:21.920
and Shift, while others prefer
to do a hybrid approach--

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.700
private and public Cloud.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.470
So how do you advise
your customers on that?

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:27.470
SONA OAKLEY: Absolutely.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:29.240
I would say that it,
again, comes back

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:30.403
to what the customer wants.

00:05:30.403 --> 00:05:31.820
You know, if most
of your business

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:33.830
is on legacy applications
and hardware,

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:36.080
we'll have to consider a
very different migration path

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:38.210
than if you're already
Cloud-native and looking

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:39.000
to scale.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.460
So for example,
we actually worked

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:42.708
with a customer who had
very aggressive timelines

00:05:42.708 --> 00:05:44.000
to shut down their data center.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.153
Again, this is a
very familiar story.

00:05:46.153 --> 00:05:48.320
And what we did was, we
advised them to kind of Lift

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:50.468
and Shift their current
applications as-is

00:05:50.468 --> 00:05:52.010
and then modernize
later because they

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:54.740
were facing a
really imminent time

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:58.220
crunch that we wanted to make
sure that we could adhere to.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.530
And then we can always
modernize those applications

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:02.990
and advise on a
containerization strategy

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:04.370
at a later point in time.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:07.218
Don't feel like you have to do
everything at the same time.

00:06:07.218 --> 00:06:09.260
STEPHANIE WONG: On the
topic of containerization,

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:12.470
people often ask, OK, how do
I know if my application is

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:15.050
a good candidate for
containerizing and pushing up

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:15.930
to the Cloud.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:17.450
Things like Dev-Test
applications,

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:20.300
multi-tier stacks,
LAMP applications,

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:23.220
or perhaps you have a Java app
running on Premise web apps--

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:23.960
how do you know?

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:24.710
SONA OAKLEY: Google
can absolutely

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:26.090
help you in that
effort-- you know,

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:28.730
especially once we've done that
categorization of applications.

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:30.480
But generally, the
ones that you mentioned

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:31.940
are good for containerization.

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:34.890
I would also include in there
are things like training labs--

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:37.350
you know, thinking, also,
about your application.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:39.620
If it's resilient to
restarts, then that

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:42.730
is a good one, as well,
to be moving over.

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:44.573
And you know, again,
it just goes back

00:06:44.573 --> 00:06:46.490
to making sure that
you're not trying to force

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:47.870
a containerization strategy.

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.030
I was working with a
large-scale manufacturing

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:53.420
company who really wanted
to go all-in on containers.

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:55.580
But due to their kind of
technical requirements,

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:57.150
it wasn't the
right fit for them.

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.540
So instead, we moved them
onto system containers,

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:01.850
which still gives them a
little bit of that flexibility

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:05.030
but isn't that full-container
portfolio that we

00:07:05.030 --> 00:07:06.853
were originally thinking about.

00:07:06.853 --> 00:07:08.270
STEPHANIE WONG:
So this still begs

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:10.850
the question, what do you do
with monolithic applications

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:13.040
because, I mean, a
lot of enterprises

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:14.180
are still running off that.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:16.370
How do you migrate to a
microservice environment?

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:17.745
SONA OAKLEY: The
way that I think

00:07:17.745 --> 00:07:19.850
about that is, if you're
thinking about scaling

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:21.270
a building or
scaling a mountain,

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:24.170
you don't try to jump all the
way up to the top at one time.

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:26.240
You know, you take it
step-by-step and really

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:27.260
go incrementally.

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:31.040
As your team learns more about
Google Cloud and Kubernetes

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:34.580
and containerization, we can
break down that application

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:37.940
and move toward more of
a microservices strategy.

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:39.920
One of the things
that Google has

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:42.260
done that's a little bit
of a nice middle ground

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:45.080
there is using
application containers.

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:47.060
So instead of thinking
about containerization

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.700
in the normal context,
an application container

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:51.710
allows you to put
the application--

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:54.060
entire application--
into a container.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:57.140
And so, that way, you can still
take advantage of the higher

00:07:57.140 --> 00:08:01.160
fault tolerance and portability
that containerization provides

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:03.980
without necessarily
moving or breaking down

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:05.660
that monolithic
application all at once.

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:08.285
STEPHANIE WONG: Right, because
I think that what's intimidating

00:08:08.285 --> 00:08:10.732
is a full rearchitecture
of these applications.

00:08:10.732 --> 00:08:11.940
SONA OAKLEY: Yes, absolutely.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:14.390
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, I love
this idea of middle ground

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:17.600
and moving in parts because it
lowers the risk and the barrier

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:19.430
to entry to Cloud migration.

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:21.260
And you can still take
advantage of, like,

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:24.980
the software-defined networking,
live migration, fast reboots.

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:27.560
But I do want to talk about
specifics a little bit--

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.410
about the tools that exist
to help you migrate, like,

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:33.140
for example, moving Compute
workloads to the Cloud.

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:34.460
Do we have anything there?

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:35.460
SONA OAKLEY: Absolutely.

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:37.346
So we have Migrate
for Compute Engine.

00:08:37.346 --> 00:08:39.179
And essentially, what
that allows you to do,

00:08:39.179 --> 00:08:42.980
whether you're moving one
workload or thousands,

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:46.280
it provides you a unified way to
move all of these componentries

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:50.750
over to Google Compute
Engine, which is GCE.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:54.320
It also provides cloud
testing and validation.

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:56.630
We have a plug-in that
makes it really simple

00:08:56.630 --> 00:09:01.370
to kind of find those
workloads and move them over.

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:03.830
And then lastly, which I think
is the most critical part--

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:05.090
and you touched on
this at the beginning--

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:06.930
is that we have a
stateful rollback.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:08.660
So if, at any point,
you feel like you

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:10.910
need to hit the ejector
button and really just get out

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:13.940
of the migration, we
have that capability,

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:16.590
where you can roll back to
your on-premise environment.

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:19.773
We can pause, take a timeout,
and see what's happening.

00:09:19.773 --> 00:09:21.690
STEPHANIE WONG: Yes, and
that's very important

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.232
because, once again,
you want to make

00:09:23.232 --> 00:09:25.670
sure you have compatibility
towards the Cloud first

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:28.910
and you're not creating
disruption for your user base.

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:31.032
So being able to roll
back is a key, I think.

00:09:31.032 --> 00:09:32.240
SONA OAKLEY: Yes, absolutely.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:34.850
One of the customer examples
that I always like to use

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:38.450
is that we had a Fortune 500
company who was using SAP,

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:39.383
actually.

00:09:39.383 --> 00:09:40.800
Implement a-- they
were originally

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:42.260
considering a
Multicloud strategy,

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:44.480
and then they moved over to GCP.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:45.950
And what we helped
them do was, we

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:50.030
helped them run SAP on
Google Cloud Platform, which

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:52.550
was really important because
they were part of the pharma

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:53.120
business.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:55.640
And that was important for
their pharma and healthcare

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:56.540
verticals.

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:59.990
We, all-in-all, I
believe covered something

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:04.700
like 7,000 systems across
30 different applications

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:08.270
with 12 terabytes of
data moving on to GCP.

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:10.805
And all of that was done with
Migrate for Compute Engine.

00:10:10.805 --> 00:10:11.930
STEPHANIE WONG: Wow, great.

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:14.362
Another great example of
a very common use case

00:10:14.362 --> 00:10:16.120
is VMware migration
to the cloud.

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:18.120
Do we have a tool that
exists for that, as well?

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:19.995
SONA OAKLEY: If you've
decided that retaining

00:10:19.995 --> 00:10:22.410
use of a VMware-based
control plane

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:24.670
is what's appropriate
for your business,

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:26.110
we can actually support that.

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:29.460
And we have support for
VMware vSphere workloads

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:31.790
that we can move over onto
Google Cloud Platform.

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:32.280
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:33.863
So what about the
applications that do

00:10:33.863 --> 00:10:35.850
need to stay
on-premise, yet they

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:38.340
want to take advantage of the
Cloud native capabilities?

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:40.170
What do we recommend
in those cases?

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:42.390
SONA OAKLEY: Earlier
in 2019, Google Cloud

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:45.300
came out with a new
product called Anthos.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:48.510
And essentially, that platform
does exactly what you asked.

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:51.750
We have seen that customers
are running their applications

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:56.070
on-premise in Google Cloud as
well as other cloud providers.

00:10:56.070 --> 00:10:58.890
And the management of
that becomes very ornery.

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:01.110
Now you're looking at
separate config managements,

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:04.310
separate monitoring platforms,
separate logging platforms.

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:07.260
And Anthos seeks to
unify all of that.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:11.940
We pull all of the underlying
hardware together and allow you

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:14.880
to manage your on-premise
and your hybrid cloud--

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:17.550
Multicloud environments
in one place.

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:19.120
If we do have a
little bit deeper,

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:21.780
the way that we do it on-premise
is that we have a tool

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:24.510
called GKE On-Premise,
which allows

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:26.550
you to implement
a containerization

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:28.830
strategy in your
on-premise environments.

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:30.413
STEPHANIE WONG: Do
you have an example

00:11:30.413 --> 00:11:33.180
of a customer that is leveraging
Anthos and using on-premise

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:34.062
and Cloud together?

00:11:34.062 --> 00:11:34.770
SONA OAKLEY: Yes.

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:37.410
Actually, we have a very
big banking customer

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:41.550
who's using Cisco HyperFlex
as well as Anthos today

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:43.780
to really do exactly that.

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:46.050
They've found that it's
been able to simplify

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:49.080
their monitoring, logging,
and config management.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:50.580
And they're still
able to get access

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:52.200
to all the benefits of Cloud.

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:55.690
And it's as if they were
deployed cloud-native,

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:57.773
but their underlying
hardware is still on-premise.

00:11:57.773 --> 00:11:59.357
STEPHANIE WONG: And
that just reminded

00:11:59.357 --> 00:12:01.580
me-- another exciting
announcement was Migrate

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:03.420
for Anthos, which
I know actually

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:06.810
lets customers move their
VM-based workloads to the cloud

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:08.370
and convert them
into containers,

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:11.640
which is amazing because you
can support edge deployments.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:14.010
You can actually migrate
from Compute Engine,

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:15.340
on-premise, other clouds.

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:18.030
So I think this is going to be
very exciting for the future,

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:18.870
as well.

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:19.530
SONA OAKLEY: Absolutely.

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:21.030
And that's actually
why we're seeing

00:12:21.030 --> 00:12:24.120
a lot of financial institutions
have interest in Anthos--

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:27.690
is because they're running these
data-driven applications, which

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:31.498
really hits that sweet spot for
containerization because, you

00:12:31.498 --> 00:12:33.540
know, instead of having
a monolithic application,

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:34.980
you can really
break it down into

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:36.180
its appropriate components.

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:39.420
And anything that you need to
put a web or mobile front-end

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:42.070
on top of really lends itself
to that use case very well.

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:44.070
STEPHANIE WONG: So for
those that are wondering,

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:47.280
how can I get started in that
decision-making process, what

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:48.300
is there to recommend?

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.700
SONA OAKLEY: Oh my gosh,
there's a number of resources.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:52.830
I would say that my
favorite is probably--

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:54.450
we have a set of
reference guides

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:56.100
available on
cloud.google.com, which

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:59.940
I think will be linked below,
that are a great Getting

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:03.480
Started guide and help you to
think through what it actually

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.490
means to migrate your business.

00:13:06.490 --> 00:13:09.190
Also, we have a variety
of professional services,

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:11.730
resources, and-- internally,
that organization

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:12.960
is called PSO.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:15.240
And so, we can absolutely
leverage workshops

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:17.720
that they have to
help you out, as well.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:18.720
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:20.637
Well, thank you so much
for being on the show.

00:13:20.637 --> 00:13:22.180
We went over a ton
of information.

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:23.030
So thanks again.

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:24.030
SONA OAKLEY: Absolutely.

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:25.302
This was a lot of fun.

00:13:25.302 --> 00:13:26.760
So thank you so
much for having me.

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:29.177
STEPHANIE WONG: Everyone, I
encourage you all to check out

00:13:29.177 --> 00:13:30.215
the resources below.

00:13:30.215 --> 00:13:32.340
And check out our other
series, "Stack Chat," where

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:35.112
we go into actual examples
and bring on customers

00:13:35.112 --> 00:13:37.320
to talk about how they're
implementing our technology

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:39.010
and migration patterns.

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:40.830
Let us know what you
